"The reason this deserves its own lesson, rather than being folded into the six excesses, is what it shows about the limits and honesty of the observational method this course has described throughout: Chinese medicine, working centuries before microorganisms could be seen or named, still noticed and separately categorized a pattern of illness that clearly spread between people rather than arising independently in each one — a genuine observation about transmission, arrived at without the tools modern medicine would later use to explain the mechanism behind it.": "2bac5ee3265a",
"This category was set apart from the ordinary six excesses specifically because it didn't behave like them. A cold that only affects people already run down by exhaustion is one kind of pattern; an illness that moves rapidly through an entire household, a school, or a town, affecting the strong and the weak alike, is described as a different kind of pattern entirely, with its own name reflecting that difference — closer to a modern epidemiologist's category than to an individual patient's constitution.": "1575f2d48670",
"This course's earlier lesson on the six excesses made a specific point: the same weather can leave one person unaffected and another person unwell, because the excess only becomes a problem at the meeting point between the environment and a particular body's resilience. Chinese medicine describes a separate category that doesn't follow this rule: the \"epidemic factor,\" a pattern recognized, centuries before germ theory existed, as spreading from person to person regardless of individual resilience, and often striking an entire community within a similar window of time.": "ba0377849ff5",
